<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jizya</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jizya was a tax levied in Islamic states on non-Muslim subjects, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmi</a>, in return for protection and exemption from military service. It was established in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> and continued in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>, where it was an important source of revenue. It was also collected in later states such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Akbar’s policy of Sulh-i-kul (“peace with all”), together with his abolition of the <b>jizya</b> on non-Muslims in 1564, reduced sectarian tensions and broadened the base of imperial support, whereas Aurangzeb re-emphasised Islamic orthodoxy, reimposed the jizya in 1679 and sponsored the legal compilation known as the Fatawa-i-Alamgiri.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>jizya</b>, a poll tax on non-Muslim subjects, generated further revenue and created a fiscal disincentive to mass conversion, since widespread conversion would have reduced income, though this was never formal policy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…a layered fiscal structure: each of the caliphate's thirty or so emirates collected taxes according to Islamic principles, including zakat on produce and livestock, <b>jizya</b> on non-Muslim subjects and kharaj on agricultural land, remitting a portion to Sokoto while retaining the remainder for local administration and military expenditure.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Raj Singh I (r. 1652–1680) opposed Aurangzeb’s reimposition of the <b>jizya</b> tax and supported the Rathores of Marwar in the Rajput war of 1679–1681, which ended in a compromise peace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>jizya</b> poll tax on non-Muslim subjects (dhimmis) and the kharaj land tax were the main sources of revenue, which created a structural tension: conversion reduced the population liable to jizya, and some governors, notably al-Hajjaj in Iraq, continued to tax converts or sent them back to the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Imperial Reform Edict of 1856 promised full legal equality for citizens of all religions and abolished the <b>jizya</b>, though a military exemption tax, the bedel-i askeri, replaced it.</blockquote><a href="/movement/tanzimat">Tanzimat · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
